lisa-design:
- Rendered is markdown-it (html: false, so raw HTML is escaped; validateLink allows only https:, http:, mailto:) then DOMPurify (no img/style/form/input/button, ALLOWED_URI_REGEXP the same schemes, a hook adds target=_blank rel="noopener noreferrer" to every link). MarkdownView.vue is the app's only v-html and binds only renderMarkdown's output.
- Both libraries load through one dynamic import on first render (cached promise), so they are their own chunks: initial JS unchanged at 123.38 KB gzip; markdown-it 41.9 KB and DOMPurify 11.7 KB gzip, loaded with the Prompts tab.
- Pinned markdown-it 15.0.3 and dompurify 3.4.17 although they are under two weeks old: the earlier versions have open advisories (GHSA-gfr2-rv44-r23c / GHSA-vfgh-jcmw-g37q for markdown-it, GHSA-cg2g-xh64-v4q7 and others for DOMPurify) that the osv scan would flag.
- Template actions stay literal: markdown-it treats {{ }} as text. Schemas have no toggle.

lisa-selfreview:
- pass 1: no blocking findings. Checked the sanitiser with specs (script tag, onerror, inline handler, javascript:/data:/relative links, images), the toggle's aria-pressed, lazy loading (separate chunks, initial bundle unchanged), npm audit --omit=dev clean.
